{
  "dom_id": "step-e12742",
  "name": "Example: Translating Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers",
  "problem": "$\\qquad$ $\\exists\\, a \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\, \\sqrt{a} >    a^2.$ \n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?\n\n- There does not exist a real number whose square root is greater than its square.\n\n- For every real number, its square root is greater than its square.\n\n- There exists a real number whose square root is greater than its square",
  "worked_solution": "The existential quantifier \"$\\exists$\" means \"there exists.\" So, our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **There exists** $a \\in \\mathbb{R}$ **such that** $\\sqrt{a} >    a^2.$ \n\nThis can be further simplified as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ There exists a real number whose square root is greater than its square.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** This statement is true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
